<ol>
<li>DOPANT SCARCITY =E2=80=94 the fiber bottleneck has an upstream chemistr=
y layer</li>
</ol>
<p>The optical-fiber boom is becoming obvious, but the less-discussed upstr=
eam constraint is germanium tetrachloride, the ultra-high-purity dopant use=
d to raise the refractive index of silica fiber cores. China remains domina=
nt in germanium production and export controls create geopolitical optional=
ity. The investment implication is that AI optics can pull on a tiny critic=
al-mineral stream whose supply is largely a by-product of other mining/refi=
ning activity.</p>
<p>New kernel primitive: byproduct_dopant_elasticity =3D incremental qualif=
ied dopant supply / incremental end-market demand. When this is low, a smal=
l optical-fiber demand shock can produce nonlinear pricing.</p>
<p>Potential exposures: Teck and Umicore are real non-Chinese germanium sou=
rces, but both are diversified; the higher-alpha task is to identify smalle=
r refiners/recyclers capable of producing optical-grade GeCl4 rather than m=
erely owning germanium-bearing feedstock.</p>
<p>Falsifiers: fiber designs materially reduce Ge loading, alternative dopa=
nts gain qualification, recycling expands rapidly, or Chinese export contro=
ls normalize enough to remove scarcity rents.</p>
